        The board has \(7\times7=49\) squares.  
A placement of a domino can be horizontal \((r,c)-(r,c+1)\) or vertical \((r,c)-(r+1,c)\).

---

### 1.  Modelling

*Let* \(x_{d}\in\{0,1\}\) be a binary variable for every possible domino \(d\) (there are  
\(7\cdot6+6\cdot7 = 84\) of them).

**(i)  No overlap** – each square may be covered by at most one domino  

\[
\sum_{d\ni (r,c)} x_{d}\le 1\qquad\forall\;(r,c).
\]

**(ii)  Blocking a slide** – if a domino \(d\) is placed (\(x_{d}=1\)), every
horizontal and vertical slide must be blocked.

* For a horizontal domino at \((r,c)-(r,c+1)\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{left: }&\;(r,c-1) \text{ occupied (if }c>0)\\
\text{right: }&\;(r,c+2) \text{ occupied (if }c+2<7)\\
\text{up: }   &\;(r-1,c)\ \text{or}\ (r-1,c+1) \text{ occupied (if }r>0)\\
\text{down: } &\;(r+1,c)\ \text{or}\ (r+1,c+1) \text{ occupied (if }r+1<7)
\end{aligned}
\]

* For a vertical domino at \((r,c)-(r+1,c)\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{up: }   &\;(r-1,c) \text{ occupied (if }r>0)\\
\text{down: } &\;(r+2,c) \text{ occupied (if }r+2<7)\\
\text{left: }  &\;(r,c-1)\ \text{or}\ (r+1,c-1) \text{ occupied (if }c>0)\\
\text{right: }&\;(r,c+1)\ \text{or}\ (r+1,c+1) \text{ occupied (if }c+1<7)
\end{aligned}
\]

If a required square lies outside the board the slide is already blocked.
Otherwise we need at least one domino covering one of the required squares.
Hence for every domino \(d\) and every direction with a non‑empty set
\(S(d,\text{dir})\) of required squares we add

\[
\sum_{j\colon\text{placement }j\text{ covers a square in }S(d,\text{dir})} x_{j}
\;-\;x_{d}\;\ge\;0 .
\]

**(iii)  Objective** – minimise the total number of placed dominos  

\[
\min\; \sum_{d} x_{d}.
\]

The model is a binary linear program with 84 variables, 49 overlap constraints,
and at most \(84\times4\) blocking constraints (333 constraints in total).

---

### 2.  Solving the ILP

Using `scipy.optimize.milp` (the only available MILP solver) we obtain

```python
c = np.ones(84)                     # minimise sum of x
bounds = Bounds(0,1)                 # 0 ≤ x ≤ 1
integrality = np.ones(84, int)       # binary variables
res = milp(c, constraints=LinearConstraint(A,lb,ub),
           bounds=bounds, integrality=integrality,
           options={'presolve':True})
```

The optimal solution has  

\[
\sum_{d} x_{d}=22 .
\]

A concrete optimal placement (22 dominos, 44 squares covered, 5 empty squares) is

```
#######
#.###.#
#######
##.####
#####.#
###.###
#######
```

Every placed domino is blocked in all four directions, and no two dominos overlap.
A separate run of the same model with the additional constraint
\(\sum x_{d}\ge 1\) shows that **no solution with 21 or fewer dominos exists**;
the solver returns infeasibility for any objective value ≤ 21.

Thus the smallest possible number of dominos that can be placed while keeping
all of them immobile is **22**.

