Question: Let $\mathcal{F}$ be the set of functions $\alpha \colon \mathbb{Z}\to \mathbb{Z}$ for which there are only finitely many $n \in \mathbb{Z}$ such that $\alpha(n) \neq 0$. For two functions $\alpha$ and $\beta$ in $\mathcal{F}$, define their product $\alpha\star\beta$ to be $\sum\limits_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} \alpha(n)\cdot \beta(n)$. Also, for $n\in\mathbb{Z}$, define a shift operator $S_n \colon \mathcal{F}\to \mathcal{F}$ by $S_n(\alpha)(t)=\alpha(t+n)$ for all $t \in \mathbb{Z}$. A function $\alpha \in \mathcal{F}$ is called shifty if $\alpha(m)=0$ for all integers $m<0$ and $m>8$ and there exists $\beta \in \mathcal{F}$ and integers $k \neq l$ such that for all $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, $S_n(\alpha)\star\beta = 1$ if $n \in \{k,l\}$ and $0$ if $n \not\in \{k,l\}$. How many shifty functions are there in $\mathcal{F}$?
